## A Bar at the Folies-Bergère: who is the barmaid?
sources:
    - id: courtauld
      title: A Bar at the Folies-Bergère
      authors: The Courtauld, London
      year: 2024
      finding: Manet's last major painting, completed in 1882 a year before he died; Suzon, a barmaid at the Folies-Bergère, posed in his studio; champagne, peppermint liqueur and Bass beer with its red triangle; the trapeze artist's green boots; a fashionable crowd in the mirror
      url: https://courtauld.ac.uk/highlights/a-bar-at-the-folies-bergere/
    - id: wiki
      title: A Bar at the Folies-Bergère
      authors: Wikipedia
      year: 2026
      finding: 'Larry L. Ligo reads the oranges as linking the barmaid with prostitution; T. J. Clark sees her as both a salesperson and a commodity; Meyers: engaged with a customer in the mirror, withdrawn face on'
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A_Bar_at_the_Folies-Berg%C3%A8re
    - id: clark
      title: 'The Painting of Modern Life: Paris in the Art of Manet and His Followers'
      authors: T. J. Clark
      year: 1985
      finding: Reads the barmaid as both a seller and part of what is on sale at the Folies-Bergère
    - id: folies
      title: Folies Bergère
      authors: Wikipedia
      year: 2026
      finding: 'Opened in 1869 (as the Folies Trévise; renamed 1872): operettas, popular songs and gymnastics'
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Folies_Berg%C3%A8re

    card title "A Bar at the Folies-Bergère"

Who is the barmaid?
    image art cites ["courtauld","wiki"] credit "Édouard Manet, 1882. Courtauld Gallery, London. Public domain." x 500 y 335 width 860 url https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Edouard_Manet%2C_A_Bar_at_the_Folies-Berg%C3%A8re.jpg/1920px-Edouard_Manet%2C_A_Bar_at_the_Folies-Berg%C3%A8re.jpg look screen regions.balcony [0,0.17,0.4,0.22] regions.barmaid [0.3,0,0.4,0.9] regions.bass [0.9,0.64,0.1,0.3] regions.behind_her [0.33,0,0.34,0.62] regions.champagne [0.12,0.62,0.15,0.32] regions.counter [0,0.62,1,0.38] regions.face [0.42,0.01,0.15,0.27] regions.front_bottles [0.02,0.63,0.25,0.31] regions.gloves [0.085,0.26,0.08,0.13] regions.lamps [0.04,0.08,0.6,0.1] regions.liqueur [0.8,0.73,0.09,0.22] regions.man [0.87,0,0.13,0.45] regions.man_face [0.88,0.06,0.12,0.17] regions.mirror [0,0,1,0.78] regions.mirror_glass [0,0,1,0.63] regions.mirror_pair [0.69,0,0.31,0.7] regions.oranges [0.645,0.66,0.18,0.28] regions.refl_bottles [0.26,0.39,0.075,0.28] regions.reflection [0.69,0.1,0.29,0.6] regions.show [0,0,0.13,0.15] regions.signature [0.022,0.79,0.05,0.1] regions.trapeze [0.02,0,0.06,0.085]

In Manet's A Bar at the Folies-Bergère, she stands at the very centre of the picture, and she is at work.
    highlight art:barmaid strength strong

In 1882, the year before he died, Édouard Manet painted her: the last big picture he finished.
    portrait manet x 795 y 420 width 200 of "Édouard Manet" cameo true

    erase manet

The Folies-Bergère was a Paris music hall, just over a decade old: songs, acrobats, and a crowd that strolled and drank.
    highlight art:balcony strength strong

    camera on ["art@[0,0,0.4,0.4]"]

    ask "Where is tonight's show? Tap it." spot show on art
        intro: The show is on right now, somewhere in this picture.
        right: Up there: a trapeze artist, and all we get are her green boots.
        wrong: Look along the very top edge of the picture.

    camera on ["art:trapeze"] zoom 5

The show gets two green boots in a corner.
    highlight art:trapeze lift true

    camera reset true

The middle of the picture goes to the woman serving the drinks.
    highlight art:barmaid lift true

    camera on ["art:face"] zoom 2

She was real: a barmaid at the Folies-Bergère known as Suzon, who came to Manet's studio and posed behind a bar he set up there.
    highlight art:face

    camera reset true

Earlier in this course, work meant fields and floors. Suzon's work is serving other people's pleasure, in the middle of the city, at night.
    highlight art:barmaid strength soft

    camera on ["art@[0.02,0.56,0.46,0.42]"]

So what is she selling? Spread on the mar (@highlight art:champagne lift true@)ble: champagne in gold foil,

    camera on ["art@[0.6,0.6,0.4,0.38]"]

English (@highlight art:bass art:liqueur@) Bass beer with its red triangle, a green peppermint liqueur,

and a glass bowl of oranges.
    highlight art:oranges

    camera reset true

Behind her hangs a huge mirror,
    highlight art:mirror_glass effect box

so the whole hall glitters at her (@highlight art:lamps art:balcony strength strong lift true@) back: the lamps, the balcony, the crowd.

    camera on ["art:balcony"] zoom 2

Out there everyone is busy enjoying themselves: top hats, bonn (@highlight art:balcony art:gloves@)ets, a woman in yellow gloves leaning on the rail.

    camera reset true

    ask "Where is the man she is serving? Tap him." intro "And she has a customer." spot man on art
        right: There, at the far right edge, in the mirror.
        wrong: He's in the mirror too: look along the right-hand edge.

He is a man in (@highlight art:man art:reflection lift true@) a top hat, seen in the mirror, and there she leans toward him, ready to serve.

Facing us, she stands straight and gives nothing away.
    highlight art:barmaid

    camera on ["art:face"] zoom 2.5

Her look is distant: the one still face in a room built for fun.
    highlight art:face strength strong rest grey

    pause 0.6

Is she bored, tired, or simply keeping her thoughts to herself? Manet doesn't say.
    highlight art:face strength soft lift true

    camera reset true

Some art historians read more into  (@highlight art:balcony lift true@)it: the hall was known for women who sold themselves,

and one even reads t (@highlight art:oranges strength strong lift true@)he oranges as a hint of that.

Another, T. J. Clark, see (@highlight art:mirror_pair strength strong lift true@)s her as both the seller

and pa (@highlight art:counter lift true@)rt of what is on sale.

Others see a worker keeping her distance from strangers, as any barmaid learns to do.
    highlight art:face lift true

Either way, the crowd behind her shows the fun;
    highlight art:balcony lift true

    camera on ["art:face"] zoom 2

her face shows the work that keeps it going.
    highlight art:face rest paper lift true

    camera reset true

Manet puts a working woman where a goddess or a queen once stood, and lets her be more than what she sells.
    highlight art:barmaid strength strong lift true

    quiz "A photographer wants to show modern city life the way Manet does. Which shot follows his method?"
        right: The still-faced cashier: a worker at the centre, the fair's fun all around her, and a face that isn't part of it.
        wrong: Manet doesn't only show a worker: think about what is behind her, and what her face does.
        + A still-faced cashier at a busy fair
        * A still-faced guest in a packed crowd
        * A smiling waiter at a busy fair
        * A close-up of a cashier counting coins

Who is the barmaid? Suzon: the one face in this glittering hall that isn't having fun.
    highlight art:face lift true

